      "status": "not_affected",
      "justification": "vulnerable_code_not_present",
      "impact_statement": "CVE-2026-3298: asyncio's ProactorEventLoop.sock_recvfrom_into() did not check nbytes against the buffer size, so the Windows-only _overlapped extension could write past the end of the buffer. Upstream fixed it in https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/148808 (commit https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/27522b7d6e6588f03e61099dd858cd5a9314e2f2), first released for 3.12 in CPython 3.12.14; see the advisory https://mail.python.org/archives/list/security-announce@python.org/thread/KWTPIQBOOOUNQP7UFSLBI437NJDFLA3F/ and https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-3298, which states that non-Windows platforms are not affected. CPython builds _overlapped only on Windows (PCbuild/_overlapped.vcxproj); configure.ac, Modules/Setup and Modules/Setup.stdlib.in in the 3.12.16 source have no entry for it. The DHI Alpine 3.23 python-3.12 package is a Linux build of CPython. Listed every file in all 528 packages of every published build (3.12.12-r0 to 3.12.16-r4) on x86_64 and aarch64: no file is named for _overlapped (the only matching names are asyncio's windows_events module, shipped as Python source and compiled bytecode, and its test module) and libpython3.12.a in every python-3.12-dev package has no such member."
    },
